  {
   "id": "conditional",
   "b": "Nothing here proves a premise, and nothing here verifies 186, 182 or 212.",
   "text": "Each proof says: if its premises hold, then its bound. This page is about the premises, not about the proofs."
  },
  {
   "id": "instances",
   "b": "Instances are not a proof.",
   "text": "The rank-four bound was decided at every prime below 2,000 and at none above. A finite check can refute a premise that was written down wrongly; it cannot prove one. For all primes the bound is Deligne’s theorem, not this run’s."
  },
  {
   "id": "replay",
   "b": "The 182’s 262 numbers were reproduced, not decided.",
   "text": "They came out of the authors’ own programs, run again by me. A replay of a claimant’s programs is never an independent decision. And the step from each computed bound to the integral it stands for is argued in the package’s comments: it is in no program and not in Lean."
  },
  {
   "id": "pairing",
   "b": "Which number belongs to which Lean row is the package’s own assignment.",
   "text": "My comparison re-derives that key from the Lean row and requires it to be equal, which checks the pairing against Lean’s own fields and no further."
  },
  {
   "id": "readings",
   "b": "The readings of the 212 are readings.",
   "text": "Two AI readers compared each quoted premise with the paper it cites. A reading is not a decision, and neither reader is a human referee."
  },
  {
   "id": "fourier",
   "b": "Not decided: the 182’s local Fourier hypothesis.",
   "text": "Its constants are free, so no finite test can refute it: a larger threshold or a larger constant escapes any instance."
  },
  {
   "id": "certificate",
   "b": "Not decided: the 212’s certificate.",
   "text": "It asserts that a certain function exists, and no such function is published. The question is open with the authors as issue #2 of their repository."
  },
  {
   "id": "inherited",
   "b": "Two of the 182’s premises are the 186’s, taken over.",
   "text": "The 182 carries a copy of the 186’s Lean file that is byte-identical, so its two shared premises inherit the checks of the 186. That its names resolve to those definitions is my reading of the file; I did not ask Lean."
  },
  {
   "id": "platform",
   "b": "The replay ran on my own build of FLINT, on Linux.",
   "text": "The package is tested by its authors on macOS with its bundled libraries. No byte of the package was changed; six things in the environment around it were, and each is listed in the records."
  },
  {
